\section{Physical Parameters, Geometric Domain, and Grid Configuration}
\label{sec:parameters_geometry}

The numerical validation and evaluation of the high-resolution single-column GSPT-SBL framework are parameterized around field campaign forcing environments (\ActiveDataset). Accurate representation of long-lived, highly stable nocturnal boundary layers requires rigorous alignment between continuous vertical boundary dynamics, substrate thermal exchange interfaces, and the underlying computational grid discretization.

\subsection{Vertical Domain Architecture and Stratification Floor}
\label{subsec:domain_stratification}

Legacy single-column models frequently restrict the computational vertical domain to shallow ceilings ($H = 100\,\mathrm{m}$ to $300\,\mathrm{m}$). However, under continuous geostrophic forcing ($U_g = \SBLParamUG\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$), shear-driven stable boundary layers and their associated Low-Level Jets (LLJs) expand vertically over typical nocturnal integration windows ($12\text{--}24\,\mathrm{h}$). Placing the domain top too low causes turbulent mixing and non-local transport closures to interact with upper boundary conditions, triggering unphysical spikes in the local Richardson number ($Ri > 10^3$) and causing solver stalling or step-rejection loops.

To eliminate upper-boundary congestion and prevent artificial wave reflections, the domain ceiling is set at:
\begin{equation}
H = 500\,\mathrm{m}.
\label{eq:domain_top}
\end{equation}

The background free-troposphere potential temperature stratification above the boundary layer is configured using a constant linear lapse rate $\gamma_\theta$. This replaces steep legacy gradients that acted as unyielding numerical walls, maintaining a well-conditioned solution manifold:
\begin{equation}
\theta(z, t=0) = \theta_a + \gamma_\theta z, \qquad \gamma_\theta = 0.004\,\mathrm{K\,m^{-1}},
\label{eq:lapse_rate}
\end{equation}
where $\theta_a = \SBLParamTA\,\mathrm{K}$ denotes the baseline reference surface air temperature.

\subsection{Discrete Grid Specification and Model Parameters}
\label{subsec:discrete_grid_spec}

For reproducible numerical integration, programmatic contract compliance, and automated solver setup, the continuous vertical domain $z \in [0, H]$ is discretized onto a regular uniform grid. Model execution is initialized under the following baseline parameter contract:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Vertical Grid Resolution ($N$):} Discretized onto $N = 250$ cell nodes, yielding a uniform vertical grid spacing of $\Delta z = 2.0\,\mathrm{m}$ across the entire $500\,\mathrm{m}$ column.
    \item \textbf{Geostrophic Forcing Vector:} Specified via constant horizontal geostrophic wind components: \texttt{Ug = \SBLParamUG}\,\,$\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$ and \texttt{Vg = \SBLParamVG}\,\,$\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$.
    \item \textbf{Coriolis Parameter ($f$):} Evaluated at campaign latitude conditions: $f = \SBLParamFCoriolis\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ (\texttt{f = \SBLParamFCoriolis}).
    \item \textbf{Surface Aerodynamic Roughness ($z_{0m}, z_{0h}$):} Roughness lengths for momentum and heat follow the active dataset configuration: $z_{0m} = \SBLParamZZeroM\,\mathrm{m}$ and $z_{0h} = \SBLParamZZeroH\,\mathrm{m}$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Substrate Thermal Coupling and Surface Boundary Conditions}
\label{subsec:surface_coupling}

The interface between the skin surface temperature ($T_s$, coded as \texttt{Ts}) and the underlying substrate core governs the surface energy balance. The conductive heat flux entering the lower boundary ($G$) is modeled via a standard linear conduction closure across a diagnostic soil depth ($d_{\mathrm{soil}} = \SBLParamDSoil\,\mathrm{m}$):
\begin{equation}
G = \lambda_s \frac{T_s - T_{\mathrm{deep}}}{d_{\mathrm{soil}}},
\label{eq:conductive_g}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_s = \SBLParamLambdaSoil\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ is the substrate thermal conductivity and $T_{\mathrm{deep}} = \SBLParamTDeep\,\mathrm{K}$ represents the unperturbed deep core temperature.

To ensure longwave radiation balance stability ($\sigma_{\mathrm{SB}} T_s^4$) during extreme clear-sky radiative cooling events ($R_{\mathrm{down}} = \SBLParamRDown\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$), runtime routines enforce the validated physical safeguard floor $T_{s,\min} = 220\,\mathrm{K}$ established in Section~\ref{subsec:ai_impl_contract}.